  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{16}(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}+\\frac{x}{10})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{16}(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{-3/4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{1}^{16}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot 4{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40({16}^{1/4}-{1}^{1/4})+\\frac{1}{20}({16}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =40(2-1)+\\frac{1}{20}(256-1) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{51}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
